/**
 * Touch input core (TASK-89, step 1) — a DOM-free virtual key source.
 *
 * Touch is synthesized as VIRTUAL KEYS: the held touch channels are projected
 * onto exactly the same key names the keyboard capture produces (lowercased
 * event.key, with 'Shift' left as-is), and the projection is merged with the
 * actual keyboard pressed set at the two prediction loops in main.tsx. The
 * entire pipeline downstream (schemes, prediction, wire mapping, server) is
 * unchanged — the loops just read a larger set.
 *
 * Design split (mirrors how the keyboard drives the game):
 * - HELD actions (movement) become virtual keys in the pressed set, read
 *   every frame by the prediction loops.
 * - DISCRETE actions (drop, interact/mine, fire, weapon, lock) stay as
 *   keydown/keyup events — the wiring tasks (91-93) call the same one-shot
 *   functions the keyboard handlers use; they are NOT virtual keys.
 *
 * Intentional collisions are safe: boost and run both map to 'Shift',
 * and vtol and jump both map to ' '. The active regime decides which loop
 * reads the set, and the schemes/loops interpret keys per regime — exactly
 * like today, where one physical Shift means 'cruise' in space and 'run'
 * on foot.
 *
 * DOM-free (no window/document) so it can be unit-tested under Node.
 */
```

